---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import WikiLayout from "../../../layouts/WikiLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Game Engines" activeNav="equipment">
  <WikiLayout title="Game Engines" activeNav="equipment" activeSidebar="game-engines">
    <p>
      Your game engine is the foundation of your project. Building a video game requires combining several tools into a smooth development pipeline. 
      Developers need software to run the game, create art, compose music, track tasks, and save code safely.
      
      A game engine is the core software framework used to build a video game. It handles rendering graphics, calculating physics, playing sounds, detecting collisions, and running scripts. 
      Choosing the right game engine depends on whether the game is 2D or 3D, which platforms it will launch on, and the developer's coding experience.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-tip">
      <strong>💡 Tip:</strong> Want a full list of game engines to look around? <a href="https://readydevstart.indiegameacademy.com/quest-logs/getting-started/">Check out our main list in the Getting Started segment</a> 
    </div>

    <h2>Engine Comparison</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Engine</th><th>Language</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Cost</th><th>Best For</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Godot 4</strong></td>
          <td>GDScript, C#</td>
          <td>Lightweight file size, open-source code, dedicated 2D tools, fast startup times</td>
          <td>Free and open source</td>
          <td>2D games, light 3D projects, fast prototyping</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Unity</strong></td>
          <td>C#</td>
          <td>Massive asset store, huge online community, easy export to almost any device</td>
          <td>Free tier; paid plans for higher revenue</td>
          <td>Cross-platform 2D/3D games, mobile releases, career development</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Unreal Engine 5</strong></td>
          <td>C++, Blueprints</td>
          <td>High-end photorealistic graphics, virtual geometry (Nanite), dynamic lighting (Lumen)</td>
          <td>Free; 5% royalty fee after $1M in sales</td>
          <td>High-budget 3D action games, cinematic visuals, large worlds</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>GameMaker</strong></td>
          <td>GML</td>
          <td>Simple drag-and-drop features, easy 2D setup, quick sprite manipulation</td>
          <td>Free non-commercial use; subscription for commercial export</td>
          <td>2D pixel art games, top-down games, rapid 2D prototypes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Defold</strong></td>
          <td>Lua</td>
          <td>Very small build file sizes, efficient mobile performance, fast load times</td>
          <td>Free</td>
          <td>Mobile 2D games, web browser games, lightweight titles</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Engine Selection Rules</h2>
    <p>
      Picking a game engine comes down to matching project needs with engine capabilities:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strongFor 2D games:</strong> Godot 4 or GameMaker offer straightforward 2D design tools and low system requirements.</li>
      <li><strong>For career opportunities and large communities:</strong> Unity provides the largest selection of tutorials and job openings across the industry.</li>
      <li><strong>For high-end 3D graphics:</strong> Unreal Engine 5 includes advanced lighting and rendering tools built directly into the editor.</li>
      <li><strong>For mobile or web browser games:</strong> Defold or Unity keep build files small and optimize memory usage for mobile phones.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout callout-tip">
      <strong>💡</strong> The best engine is the one you actually learn and ship with. Don't engine-hop: Pick one and commit for your first project. 

      Sticking with one engine throughout a project's lifecycle prevents delays, as switching engines mid-production requires rebuilding code and game assets.
    </div>
  </WikiLayout>
</BaseLayout>
